---
description: Prefer top-level imports; ban noqa PLC0415 for routine local imports
globs: "**/*.py"
alwaysApply: false
---

# hsk py imports

Put imports at the top of the module. Do not silence `PLC0415` with `# noqa` to keep a local import.

## Default (required)

```python
# ❌ BAD — local import + noqa just to satisfy ruff
def _is_ordered_list_line(line: str) -> bool:
    import re  # noqa: PLC0415

    return bool(re.match(r"^\s*\d+[.)]\s", line))

# ✅ GOOD — top-level import
import re

def _is_ordered_list_line(line: str) -> bool:
    return bool(re.match(r"^\s*\d+[.)]\s", line))
```

Same for stdlib and normal third-party / project modules: move the import to the top of the file.

## Allowed local imports (rare)

A local `import` with `# noqa: PLC0415` is allowed only when there is a real reason:

- circular import that cannot be resolved by restructuring
- optional / heavy dependency that must not load at module import time (e.g. `easyocr`, `pillow_avif`, `clr`)
- intentional lazy load to avoid a costly or platform-specific import on every startup

If none of those apply, fix `PLC0415` by moving the import to the top — never with `# noqa: PLC0415`.
